mbers with XOR0x33 and validates exact lengths, SECO magic and embedded SHA256. E-176 independently reproduces restoration and seed equality with the authenticated cached original. E-197 independently confirms complete two-member ZIP reading, restored-byte equality and ordered SHA1 pair; E-199 covers complete-set selection. E-230 independently observes the corresponding XOR0x33 implementation in cached desktop executable code, corroborating the transform but not changing either original hash. Order is seed.seco then storage.seco; assumes given P-1 synthetic laptop premise. — by s42120103
7718-- **#262** question:6 (established): `services.exe_172.30.145.189:6969` — the malware-identifying artifact established by the evidence is the recovered Desktop `services.exe`; no malware-family name is established. The unmatched final `)` in the requested format is treated as punctuation rather than part of the value pending C-2. **(superseded by #264)** _(high: The answer rests on exact cached-file ownership, PE section mapping, RIP-relative string references, Go function boundaries, machine-code call targets, and an independently reconstructed control-flow chain. The filename/endpoint and wallet/XOR relationships are directly and independently reproduced; the only remaining uncertainty concerns exact library symbol names, malware-family naming, execution, and successful transmission, none of which is asserted.)_ — rests on: E-211, E-218, E-230, E-245, E-235, E-234, E-250 — contrary: E-217, E-243 — still open: No specific malware-family label is established because the sparse cached image lacks complete custom-symbol/family metadata. Successful execution, connection, and transfer remain outside the static claim. C-2 remains open only on whether the requested display format intended a literal trailing parenthesis and whether 'malware' demanded a family rather than the recoverable filename. — would change it: A complete authenticated executable showing a different file attribution, intact symbols that reverse the wallet/network call interpretation, or an operator clarification requiring a family label rather than the recovered filename would change or narrow the answer. — parts: malware "Identify the malware artifact that targets the cryptocurrency wallet" established (E-211, E-218, E-230, E-235, E-245, E-250); destination "Identify the destination IP address and port" established (E-218, E-234, E-235, E-245, E-250); intent "Establish that the artifact's code links wallet targeting to attempted network transmission intent" established (E-230, E-235, E-245) — the premise tested against "the question's premise is not supported": Static analysis supports the proposition that the recovered Desktop services.exe targets the Exodus wallet and prepares transformed content for TCP transmission to the embedded endpoint; it does not show that execution or transmission succeeded. (E-235, E-245) — reasoning: E-211 and E-218 structurally attribute the cached Go PE, wallet literal, and destination literal to the distinct Desktop `services.exe`, not Windows System32 `services.exe`. E-230 and independently reproduced E-245 connect the wallet-bearing function to the XOR-0x33 helper. E-235 maps the wallet path, payload preparation, endpoint, and TCP/dial-shaped helper into one offset-anchored static control-flow chain, while E-234 and E-250 independently verify the decisive string references and destination bytes. This establishes the recovered executable filename and intended destination as requested, but only as static capability/intent: it does not establish successful execution, connection, or transfer. E-217's contiguous physical-PE scan did not link the scattered pages, and E-243 did not associate the endpoint with the legitimate Exodus process; those narrower negative routes do not contradict the later CONTROL_AREA/file-object mapping and cached-PE static chain. — by s42120106
